-- title:
--   Puder, Claim 3.1 — every w-critical subgroup is an algebraic extension of ⟨w⟩
-- statement:
--   Let $w \in \mathbf F_d$ and let $J$ be a $w$-critical subgroup, $J \in \mathrm{Crit}(w)$ (`Puder.Crit`: $J$ contains $w$, $w$ is not primitive in $J$, and $J$ is free of finite rank $\pi(w)$). Then $J$ is an algebraic extension of the cyclic subgroup $\langle w \rangle$ (`Subgroup.zpowers w`): $\langle w \rangle \le J$ and no subgroup $L$ with $\langle w \rangle \le L \lneq J$ is a free factor of $J$ (`Puder.IsAlgExt`, `Puder.IsFreeFactor`).
--
--   Puder, p. 12: “**Claim 3.1.** [Pud14, Cor. 4.4] *Every $w$-critical subgroup is an algebraic extension of $\langle w \rangle$ (the subgroup generated by $w$).*
--   *More precisely, $\mathrm{Crit}(w)$ consists precisely of the algebraic extensions of $\langle w \rangle$ of minimal rank besides $\langle w \rangle$ itself.*” The footnote to the second sentence reads “Unless $w = 1$ in which case $\mathrm{Crit}(w) = \{\langle\rangle\} = \{\langle w \rangle\}$.”
--
--   *Formalization note.* Only the first sentence is stated; it is the one the proof of Proposition 4.3 uses, in (4.1). The second sentence, the characterization of $\mathrm{Crit}(w)$ as the algebraic extensions of minimal rank, is not stated.
--
--   Source: D. Puder, *Expansion of random graphs: new proofs, new results*, Invent. Math. 201 (2015) 845–908, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00222-014-0560-x (arXiv:1212.5216, p. 12), Claim 3.1, which cites D. Puder, *Primitive words, free factors and measure preservation*, Israel J. Math. 201 (2014) 25–73, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11856-013-0055-2, Corollary 4.4.
-- source:
--   Puder, D., Expansion of random graphs: new proofs, new results, Invent. Math. 201 (2015) 845–908, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00222-014-0560-x (arXiv:1212.5216), p. 12, Claim 3.1 (first sentence), which cites Puder, D., Primitive words, free factors and measure preservation, Israel J. Math. 201 (2014) 25–73, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11856-013-0055-2 (arXiv:1104.3991), Corollary 4.4

